WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.247
`Box<T>` is the most straightforward Rust
smart pointer.

2
00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:06.354
It stores a value on the heap and gives
that value one clear owner.

3
00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:08.789
The owner can move, pass, return, or store
the box,

4
00:00:08.869 --> 00:00:11.392
but the boxed value still has a single
ownership path.

5
00:00:11.472 --> 00:00:15.340
That simplicity makes `Box<T>` easier to
reason about than reference counting or

6
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.485
interior mutability.

7
00:00:16.565 --> 00:00:20.321
Owned heap indirection is useful when a
type needs a stable size at compile time,

8
00:00:20.401 --> 00:00:24.045
such as recursive structures where a value
contains another value of the same

9
00:00:24.125 --> 00:00:24.854
logical shape.

10
00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:28.690
A box can also be useful for large values
that should not be moved around by value,

11
00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.909
or for APIs that need owned indirection
without shared ownership.

12
00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:34.008
`Box<T>` also appears with trait objects,

13
00:00:34.088 --> 00:00:37.732
where code owns a value through a trait
interface rather than a concrete type.

14
00:00:37.812 --> 00:00:41.792
Used carefully, this can make plugin-like
or strategy-style APIs easier to express

15
00:00:41.872 --> 00:00:43.385
while keeping ownership clear.

16
00:00:43.465 --> 00:00:46.493
The caller owns the boxed object, and
cleanup follows the owner.

17
00:00:46.573 --> 00:00:49.993
When the owner of a `Box<T>` goes out of
scope, the boxed value is dropped.

18
00:00:50.073 --> 00:00:52.763
There is no reference count and no hidden
group of owners.

19
00:00:52.843 --> 00:00:56.319
Reviewers can follow the owner to
understand when cleanup should happen,

20
00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:59.651
which is why `Box<T>` is often a good
first smart pointer to consider.

21
00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:03.319
Defensive Rust design usually starts with
ordinary ownership, borrowing,

22
00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:05.529
or `Box<T>` before adding shared
ownership.

23
00:01:05.609 --> 00:01:08.973
If a value does not need multiple owners,
do not add reference counting.

24
00:01:09.053 --> 00:01:12.585
If it does not need runtime mutation
rules, do not add interior mutability.

25
00:01:12.665 --> 00:01:15.860
Simple ownership is often the safest and
most maintainable design.
